[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-10871"] :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}+x)=4x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(1)=4(1)+1=5,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{5}(x-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{1}{5}+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{16}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{16}{5} & =0 \\\\ x & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(16,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-47408"] :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{5}-2x.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{5}-2x)=10{x}^{4}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1)=10(1)^{4}-2=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =-\\frac{1}{8}(x-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{8}+\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a+b=-\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{8}=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-103546/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "dd66e824-ede1-5352-a635-86372541d76d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103546/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "ef119395-e0b3-5ba8-93f6-995c3da1f6cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103546/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "b52e8744-54f9-5ff3-86c1-455fcd4db1d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103546/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer/id #uuid "578b5719-0cac-5261-a927-7d4e10f39141" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103546/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "4a27c8ce-7896-529a-94ce-b4ecc3a15302" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103546/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0f5a2264-cb7f-53b9-9781-3b162d6a86ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-103546/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer-field/choices ["q-103546/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" "q-103546/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-103546/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" "q-103546/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" "q-103546/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-103546" :question/id #uuid "b47ace4f-daa7-598e-bbed-f1675b554697" :question/math-academy-id "q-103546" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}$ at the point $(1,4)$ is given by $x+py=q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the value of $q-p.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1)=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{1}^{3}}}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ 2y-8 & =x-1 \\\\ x-2y & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q-p=-7-(-2)=-5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-103546/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a323ba-f2a7-5a49-abd4-a9812518ef8c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-103546"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-308823/field-1/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer/id #uuid "c62c4e05-1829-5405-9851-68156c959f02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-308823/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "dc778d3b-c76e-5b55-9f39-91e81a4665e5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-308823/field-1/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer-field/choices ["q-308823/field-1/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-308823/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "1c768d91-fad4-581e-85b7-08bc025ad0db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-308823/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "4534d582-f3a2-5fbf-9714-ac9aa89ce12a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-308823/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-308823/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-308823" :question/id #uuid "f216eb4b-9d53-5b6d-b67a-8fb8e17cde01" :question/math-academy-id "q-308823" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{2}{x}+1$ at the point $(1,3)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe normal line intersects the $x$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{x}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{-1}+1) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(1)=-\\frac{2}{(1)^{2}}=-2,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the  $x$-axis at $(\\,-5,0\\,).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-308823/field-1" "q-308823/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ba61258-92a9-511e-891f-9e6adce2edb0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-308823"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342153/field-1/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" :answer/id #uuid "755785d3-b212-5cf0-92cd-6a1e0b14657a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342153/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "d748d196-19c3-5676-b153-1d4c82fecd7f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-342153/field-1/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" :answer-field/choices ["q-342153/field-1/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342153" :question/id #uuid "ef7987ad-e618-5dff-80f2-97375df03f78" :question/math-academy-id "q-342153" :question/problem "Find the slope ${m}^{′}$ of the normal line to the curve $y=4-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{4},2).$\n\n${m}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=4-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4-{x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{1}{4},2):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{4})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3}}}=4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope ${m}^{′}$ of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{′} & =-\\frac{1}{m} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342153/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a05f6c0-98ab-5197-922e-8e8dff1d4302"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342153"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
